        <div id="cpap-watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Watts Does a CPAP Use? 30 to 60 Without the Humidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number that decides which power station you need is your CPAP's average draw across a night, and it is lower than most people expect. A CPAP without heated humidification typically averages somewhere around 30 to 60 watts, and many modern machines run well below the rating printed on their power supply. The power brick's rating is a maximum for the worst case, not what the machine pulls hour after hour. Heated humidification changes the picture completely, because heating water is the largest single load in the system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Without the Humidifier</h3>

          <p>
            With the humidifier off, the blower is the only meaningful load. Draw rises with pressure, so a machine set at 8 cmH2O uses less than the same machine at 15. Mask leaks raise it too, because the blower works harder to hold pressure. That is why two people with the same model can see very different runtimes from the same battery, and why any runtime chart, including ours, is an estimate rather than a promise.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">With a Heated Humidifier and Tube</h3>

          <p>
            Turn on the heated humidifier and a heated tube and the average draw can roughly double or more, often landing near 90 watts and sometimes above 100 in a cold room. This is the single biggest lever you control. A station that covers three nights dry may cover just one with the humidifier on, and a 300Wh station that gets you through a night dry will not get you to morning with heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Find Your Own Number</h3>

          <p>
            Before you buy, find your machine's real figure. The manufacturer's battery guide or user manual usually lists typical consumption at different pressures, and a plug-in watt meter on the wall outlet for one night gives you a measured average for your own settings. Divide the total watt-hours the meter reports by the hours you slept and you have the number to use in the next section. Our full walkthrough of the arithmetic is in <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">can a portable power station run a CPAP</a>; this guide is about choosing the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nights-per-charge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Nights Per Charge: Divide Usable Watt-Hours by One Night's Draw
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every runtime figure on this page comes from one formula applied to the capacity each listing states. None of them is a measured result. Nights per charge is roughly the rated watt-hours, times an efficiency factor, divided by your machine's average watts times the hours you sleep.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Formula</h3>

          <p>
            Nights equals rated Wh times 0.85, divided by average watts times 8 hours. The 0.85 covers the inverter's conversion loss and the station's own electronics, which run all night. A 1,024Wh station therefore gives about 870 usable watt-hours. At a 40W average, one eight-hour night uses 320Wh, so the estimate is about 2.7 nights. At 90W with heated humidification, one night uses 720Wh, so the estimate is about 1.2 nights.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Estimates for These Picks</h3>

          <p>
            Applied to the stated capacities, the 1,024Wh and 1,004.8Wh stations land at roughly 5.4 nights at a 20W average, 2.7 nights at 40W and 1.2 nights at 90W. The 288Wh and 292Wh stations land at roughly 1.5 nights at 20W, 0.8 nights at 40W and about a third of a night at 90W. Treat those as planning numbers, then replace the wattage with your own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Real Runtime Differs</h3>

          <p>
            Cold rooms cut usable capacity and raise humidifier draw. An older battery holds less than its rating. Running a phone charger or a lamp from the same station takes its share. On the other side, a DC cable avoids most of the inverter loss, so DC runtime usually beats the AC estimate. Build in a margin: if the math says 1.1 nights, plan on one night and recharge by day.
          </p>

