        <div id="dc-vs-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. DC Adapter or AC Inverter? DC Gets More Nights From the Same Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            A CPAP plugged into a station's AC outlet goes through two conversions: the station turns battery DC into 120V AC, then the CPAP's own power brick turns it back into low-voltage DC. Each step loses energy, and the inverter also draws power just by being on. A DC cable from the station's 12V car port to the machine skips both steps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why AC Costs You Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            On a heavy load the inverter's losses are a small slice. On a 30 to 40 watt CPAP they are a much bigger share of the total, because the inverter's own idle draw does not shrink with the load. That is why the same station can deliver noticeably more hours through a DC cable than through an AC outlet with the same machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What You Need for DC</h3>

          <p>
            Most major CPAP makers sell a 12V DC power cable or converter for their machines, usually listed as a separate accessory. Check that it matches your model exactly, and check the station's car port rating: the Jackery Explorer 300 and Anker SOLIX C300 DC both list a 120W car port, comfortably above a CPAP's draw. Some machines cannot run the heated humidifier on DC, so read the cable's notes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When AC Is Fine</h3>

          <p>
            If you bought a 1kWh station, AC is simpler and the loss rarely matters, because you have capacity to spare. AC is also the only way to use a UPS switchover at home, since pass-through applies to the AC outlets. Use DC when capacity is tight, such as a 300Wh station on a camping trip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pure-sine-wave" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does a CPAP Need a Pure Sine Wave Inverter? Yes, Buy Nothing Else
          </h2>

          <p>
            A CPAP's power brick expects clean, smooth AC like the grid supplies. A pure sine wave inverter reproduces that waveform. A modified sine wave inverter produces a stepped approximation that can make power supplies run hot, hum or fail to start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Check on the Listing</h3>

          <p>
            Look for the words pure sine wave on the listing or spec sheet. Jackery's listing for the Explorer 1000 v2 states it outright for its AC outlets. Mainstream power stations from the large brands are generally built around pure sine wave inverters, but confirm it for the exact model you buy rather than assuming. The cheapest no-name stations and many car inverters are where modified sine wave still turns up. Our explainer on <a href="/pure-sine-wave-vs-modified-sine-wave/">pure vs modified sine wave</a> covers the difference in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why It Matters More for Medical Devices</h3>

          <p>
            A phone charger will usually shrug off a rough waveform. A medical device that runs eight hours a night, every night, is not something to gamble on. If your CPAP manufacturer publishes guidance on inverters or battery use, follow it; some name the specific battery or power accessories they support.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DC Sidesteps the Question</h3>

          <p>
            If you run the CPAP through a DC cable, the inverter is not involved and the waveform does not matter. That is one more reason a DC-only station like the Anker SOLIX C300 DC can make sense, provided your machine has a compatible cable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ups-pass-through" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. UPS Pass-Through: Will the CPAP Keep Running When the Power Cuts?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A UPS or pass-through feature lets the station sit between the wall and the CPAP every night. While the grid is up, the CPAP runs from the wall through the station. When the grid drops, the station switches to battery. If the switch is fast enough, the CPAP never notices and you never wake.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Switchover Time</h3>

          <p>
            The listings here quote switchover times: 10ms for the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2, BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 and Elite 100 Mini, and under 10ms for the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Classic. The Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 is positioned for UPS use without a stated switchover time, and the Jackery Explorer 300 lists no UPS function. A dedicated online UPS switches with zero gap; power stations are close but not identical, so treat the number as a guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test It Before You Need It</h3>

          <p>
            On a quiet evening, run the CPAP through the station and switch the wall outlet off. If the machine keeps blowing without restarting, the switchover works with your model. If it resets, you know now rather than at 3 a.m. in a storm, and you can plan to run from battery on nights when outages are likely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leaving It Plugged In Every Night</h3>

          <p>
            Running pass-through every night keeps the battery near full, which is what you want for an outage. Some stations let you set a charge limit in the app to reduce long-term wear. LiFePO4 cells tolerate this kind of use better than older lithium-ion chemistries, which is another reason every pick here uses LFP.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fan-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fan Noise at Night: Small Loads Keep Most Stations Quiet
          </h2>

          <p>
            A station beside the bed has to be quiet. The good news is that a CPAP is a light load, and stations mostly spin their cooling fans when they are charging fast or delivering heavy power, not when they are trickling 40 watts to a nightstand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Fans Run</h3>

          <p>
            Fans come on with heat. Fast AC charging is the loudest case, which is why Jackery's app offers a 30 dB quiet overnight charging mode on the Explorer 1000 v2. Heavy loads like a kettle or microwave are the next. With only a CPAP attached, owners commonly report the fan stays off or runs only occasionally, though that varies by model and room temperature.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charging Overnight Versus Running Overnight</h3>

          <p>
            If the station is recharging while it powers the CPAP, expect more fan noise than on battery alone. Use a slow or quiet charge mode at night, or charge during the day and run from battery while you sleep. Pass-through use on a UPS-capable station usually keeps charging gentle once the battery is full.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the station on a hard surface with clear space around its vents, not on carpet or wedged against bedding. Good airflow keeps the fan from needing to spin, and keeps the battery cool. Our guide to <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">whether power stations are safe indoors</a> covers placement more fully.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifepo4" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. LiFePO4 Batteries: The Right Chemistry for Nightly CPAP Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            A CPAP station may cycle every night for years, so cycle life matters more than it does for an occasional camping battery. All seven picks list LiFePO4 or LFP cells, the same chemistry, and the listings rate them for thousands of cycles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cycle Life in Real Terms</h3>

          <p>
            Jackery rates the Explorer 1000 v2 for 4,000 cycles to 70% capacity, Anker rates the SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 for 4,000 cycles to at least 80%, and BLUETTI rates the Elite 100 Mini for 6,000-plus cycles. Even used every night, that is many years before capacity falls noticeably. Older nickel-manganese-cobalt stations were often rated for far fewer cycles. Our comparison of <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion power stations</a> explains the trade-offs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight Is the Cost</h3>

          <p>
            LiFePO4 is heavier per watt-hour. That is why a 1kWh station is a two-handed lift and the 300Wh class stays under 10 lb. For a bedside unit the weight is irrelevant; for camping, it decides which size you will actually carry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Rooms and Cold Tents</h3>

          <p>
            All lithium batteries deliver less in the cold, and most stations limit charging below freezing. In a winter outage or a cold campsite, keep the station inside the warm part of the room or tent, and plan on fewer nights than the estimate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recharging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Recharging Between Nights: Wall, Generator, Car and Solar
          </h2>

          <p>
            In a long outage the question is not just how many nights one charge lasts, but how fast you can refill it by day. That is where fast-charging stations earn their place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wall or Generator</h3>

          <p>
            The 1kWh picks recharge quickly from AC: Anker lists about 49 minutes for the SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 in its fast mode, EcoFlow lists 0 to 80% in about 45 minutes for the DELTA 3 Classic, and Jackery lists a full charge in about 50 minutes in emergency mode. An hour on a generator, or at a friend's or a warming center outlet, restores several nights of CPAP use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar</h3>

          <p>
            Solar turns a station into an indefinite CPAP supply in a long summer outage. The SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 accepts up to 600W of solar, and the DELTA 3 Classic lists a 2.5-hour recharge from a 500W panel. Small stations take smaller panels: Jackery lists the Explorer 300 reaching 80% in about 2.8 hours from 100W. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a power station with solar</a> covers panel sizing. A <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank</a> is useful for keeping phones alive, but it is far too small to run a CPAP.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flying With a CPAP Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Airline rules limit lithium batteries to 160Wh without airline approval, and above 160Wh they are not allowed on passenger flights at all. Every station here is larger than that, so none of them can fly. Travelers need a purpose-made CPAP battery under 100Wh or 160Wh instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Building a CPAP Outage Plan Around the Station
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power station is the core of a CPAP backup plan, not the whole of it. A few simple steps make the difference between a station that works on the night and one that is flat in a closet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Charged and Ready</h3>

          <p>
            Store the station above about half charge, top it up every couple of months, and keep the CPAP's DC cable or AC cord with it. If you use pass-through, it is always full. Write your machine's estimated nights on a label so whoever is home knows how long it lasts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tell Your Utility and Supplier</h3>

          <p>
            Many utilities run medical priority or medical baseline programs for customers who depend on powered medical equipment. Registration does not guarantee power, but it can mean earlier warnings of planned shutoffs. Your equipment supplier can also confirm which batteries and cables your machine supports.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size Up for the Rest of the House</h3>

          <p>
            If you also want lights, a router and a fridge, look at our broader <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> list and our guide to <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">power stations for home backup</a>. For a whole-house outage plan, a station for the CPAP plus a generator outside for heavy loads is a common, sensible setup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by the Power Brick Rating Instead of Real Draw</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The wattage printed on a CPAP's power supply is a maximum, not the average. Sizing a station from it overstates what you need for dry nights, while forgetting the humidifier understates it. Measure or look up your machine's average draw at your pressure, with and without humidification, and size from that.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting Runtime Claims Without Doing the Math</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Runtime claims assume a particular machine, pressure and humidifier setting that may not match yours. Take the rated watt-hours, multiply by about 0.85, and divide by your machine's average watts times your hours of sleep. If the answer is close to one night, treat it as less than one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Humidifier on a Small Battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heated humidification can more than double a CPAP's draw. On a 300Wh station that turns a full night into a few hours. In an outage or on a camping trip with a small station, switch the humidifier and heated tube off, or buy a 1kWh station if you cannot sleep without them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Testing the UPS Switchover</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 10ms switchover is fast, but whether your machine rides through it is something to confirm at home. Run the CPAP through the station and switch the wall outlet off. Finding out during a real storm, when the machine restarts at 3 a.m., is the worst time to learn.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Station Flat in a Closet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A station stored empty for months is no help when the power goes. Keep it above about half charge, top it up every couple of months, store the CPAP's cable with it, or use it on pass-through every night so it is always full when an outage hits.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

