        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best power station for CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2. Its listing specifies 1,024Wh of LiFePO4 capacity, pure sine wave AC outlets, a 10ms UPS switchover and a quiet overnight charging mode. By our estimate that covers roughly 2.7 nights at a 40W average draw. For one-night camping, the BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 or Jackery Explorer 300 is lighter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hours will a 1000Wh power station run a CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Multiply 1,000Wh by about 0.85 for usable energy, about 850Wh, then divide by your machine's average watts. At 40 watts that is roughly 21 hours, or about two and a half eight-hour nights. At 90 watts with heated humidification it is roughly nine hours. These are estimates, so check your machine's own consumption figure and keep a margin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 300Wh power station run a CPAP all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, if the humidifier is off. A 300Wh station gives about 255 usable watt-hours by our estimate, which covers an eight-hour night at an average draw of about 32 watts or less. Many machines average less than that without humidification, especially through a DC cable. With heated humidification, a 300Wh station will not last the night.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a DC cable or the AC outlet for my CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a DC cable when capacity is tight. It skips the inverter, which wastes energy converting battery power to AC and then back to DC inside the CPAP's power supply. The gain is largest on small stations. Use AC on a large station or when you want UPS pass-through at home, since switchover applies to the AC outlets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a CPAP need a pure sine wave inverter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A CPAP's power supply is designed for clean grid-style AC, and a modified sine wave inverter can make it run hot, hum or fail to start. Confirm pure sine wave on the spec sheet of the exact station you buy. If you power the CPAP through a DC cable, the inverter is bypassed and the waveform no longer matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take a portable power station on a plane for my CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not one of these. Airline rules allow lithium batteries up to 100Wh freely and 100 to 160Wh with airline approval, and ban larger batteries on passenger aircraft. Every station in this guide is well over 160Wh. Travelers should buy a CPAP battery designed for flight and check their airline's medical device rules before the trip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to leave a CPAP plugged into a power station every night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on a station that lists UPS or pass-through charging, which is what the feature is for. Keep the station on a hard surface with clear vents, away from bedding. LiFePO4 batteries tolerate nightly cycling well. Test the switchover once by turning the wall outlet off while the CPAP runs, so you know your machine rides through it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

